y will face a very powerful  reaction from afghan citizens and also  our forces we will filter them out and  will destroy their hideouts  we have to make it very clear that there  has been no claim of responsibility of  the attack here in kabul but this is a  group isil in harastan islamic state of  the iraq and iraq and levant in horison  province which is the wider afghanistan  central asian region which has been  carrying out these attacks in jalalabad  and nangarhar taliban have been  insisting that they are going after them  and they say they are going to be true  to the pledge to the rest of the world  that afghan soil is not going to be used  by any group against any other country  let alone afghan people so it is hugely  significant that this attack in the  heart of the capital kabul at a  gathering which was secured by taliban  fighters has taken place and now taliban  are going to have to tell  not just the afghan people but their  supporters as well about the  significance of the threat they face and  what are they going to do about it okay  osama bin javid that bring us up to date  from the afghan capital  osama thank you very much indeed  well let's get more on this now from  fahad nabil he joins us from islamabad  who's the security analyst and  researcher on militancy it's good to  speak to you i'm just going to repeat  what our correspondence said there has  been  no claim of responsibility for this  attack but given the context  of the situation that we're in now  which groups would you say would be  among the the first that you would  suspect  who i mean who  do you think would be attacking it looks  like the taliban themselves are being  attacked  well one cannot rule out the possibility  that islamic state horizon province  might possibly be behind this attack  however so far they have not officially  claimed this attack but there's a strong  likelihood that they might be behind  this attack and in terms of the  significance of this uh this is the  first time the taliban have been  attacked it is the first time since the  afghan capital was attacked since the  taliban takeover  what do you think this speaks in terms  of the threat level for  for civilians in afghanistan because  again the the taliban are meant to be  ensuring security and with bomb attacks  and mosque attacks it looks like i mean  this will have many people uh on edge  really it almost seems like the war  isn't over  ever since the taliban takeover we have  seen a surge of activity by islamic  state for some province however this is  the first high profile attack since the  kabul airport track by slang straight  for some prevents so these attacks the  continue frequency of these attacks is  really putting a serious question on the  capabilities of afghan talent to protect  not only  kabul which is the capital but all  across afghanistan as well and so what  how would you assess the taliban's  ability to to protect civilians and to  protect themselves from the type of  attacks which when they were at war as  part of the civil war they were carrying  out does this give them an advantage if  they're using the same  uh  their adversaries now or using the same  tactics that arguably they use  uh yes definitely of one taliban now if  it's the same threat which they used to  pose to the foreign security forces or  the foreign troops in afghanistan so it  is important for them to realize this  fact that they have to move from this  insurgency mode to now governance world  where they have to ensure they have to  isolate locate the slipper cells of  islamic state for sanctuaries there have  been an operation a few days ago in  kabul so they have to continue to locate  isolate the the elements of slangster  horizon province in order to deal with  this threat and do you think they will  be able to deal with that they obviously  have a country to run which th they're  learning how to do very quickly they've  been thrown in the deep end if you like  when